88b59370075aff7735baffff4b56628","sm":"FILL"}],"hs":"12171wp6f2o"},{"id":"I259:38778;355:37756","nm":"glint","ty":"R","w":91.52,"h":686.4,"x":-318.032,"y":-200.2,"tf":[0.927188,-0.374595,0.374595,0.927188],"op":0.14,"fl":[{"t":"L","hp":[[0,0.5],[1,0.5],[0,1]],"st":[[0,255,255,255,0],[0.5,255,255,255,0.85],[1,255,255,255,0]]}],"hs":"1b77o94ed5t"}],"hs":"p2ujsntrpq"}],"hs":"1nxuv7d900x"},{"id":"236:47926","nm":"I WAS NEVER JUST ONE THING. Hi. I'm Veer. And honestly, I've spent most of my life trying to figure out who I want to become. When I was a kid, I wanted to be a scientist. I wanted to understand how things worked, why things existed, and what was possible beyond what we already knew. But there was another side of me. I grew up in a family of Vishwakarma goldsmiths, where creativity wasn't just a hobby; it was a craft passed down through generations. People often said creativity was in our DNA. Maybe they were right, because even as a kid, I was always making something. Sketching, drawing, performing on stage, dancing, acting in skits, building random things out of paper, and creating my own little worlds out of whatever I could find. I loved bringing ideas to life. I was good at studies, too. I did well in school, played cricket, learned martial arts from my father, and kept finding new things to get obsessed with. Then came engineering: computer science. A path that seemed to make sense for the future I was walking into. And I did well. I graduated with an 8.7 GPA. But somewhere along the way, I started asking myself a question: What do I actually want to do with my life? I was interested in too many things: technology, design, art, products, business, and creativity. I'd get excited about one thing, explore it, learn it, and then discover something else that fascinated me. Eventually, I came across that familiar phrase: A jack of all trades, master of none. For a while, I wondered if that was me. Was I someone who could do a little bit of everything but never become exceptional at anything? But the more I thought about it, the more I realized something. I've never really stopped creating. Even when life changed, my interests evolved, or I wasn't sure where I was going, that part of me stayed. I went from drawing on paper to designing digital experiences, from building things with my hands to exploring what I could build with technology, and from wondering how the world works to wondering how I could make it work better. Now, we're entering a world where artificial intelligence is changing almost everything: the way we think, work, and create. I don't want to just watch that transformation happen. I want to be part of it. I want to understand technology, design products people love, solve meaningful problems, and build things that didn't exist before. Because for me, design isn't just about making something look good. It's about imagining something better and finding a way to bring it into existence. So, who am I? I'm Veer. Yaswanth Veer. I'm still learning, experimenting, and figuring things out. I don't have every answer, and I don't know exactly where this journey will take me. But maybe I don't need to have it all figured out just yet. Maybe the goal isn't to choose one version of myself and leave the rest behind. Maybe it's to bring everything I've learned, everything I've explored, and everything I'm passionate about into the things I create. I'm here to design. I'm here to build. I'm here to turn ideas into something real. And this time, instead of wondering what I might become, I'm going to find out. I'm Veer. And this is just the beginning.","ty":"T","w":1347,"h":2150,"x":46,"y":2614,"fl":[{"t":"S","c":[255,255,255,1]}],"tx":{"c":"I WAS NEVER JUST ONE THING.\nHi. I'm Veer. And honestly, I've spent most of my life trying to figure out who I want to become. When I was a kid, I wanted to be a scientist. I wanted to understand how things worked, why things existed, and what was possible beyond what we already knew.\nBut there was another side of me. I grew up in a family of Vishwakarma goldsmiths, where creativity wasn't just a hobby; it was a craft passed down through generations. People often said creativity was in our DNA. Maybe they were right, because even as a kid, I was always making something. Sketching, drawing, performing on stage, dancing, acting in skits, building random things out of paper, and creating my own little worlds out of whatever I could find. I loved bringing ideas to life.\nI was good at studies, too. I did well in school, played cricket, learned martial arts from my father, and kept finding new things to get obsessed with. Then came engineering: computer science. A path that seemed to make sense for the future I was walking into. And I did well. I graduated with an 8.7 GPA.\nBut somewhere along the way, I started asking myself a question: What do I actually want to do with my life? I was interested in too many things: technology, design, art, products, business, and creativity. I'd get excited about one thing, explore it, learn it, and then discover something else that fascinated me. Eventually, I came across that familiar phrase: A jack of all trades, master of none. For a while, I wondered if that was me. Was I someone who could do a little bit of everything but never become exceptional at anything?\nBut the more I thought about it, the more I realized something. I've never really stopped creating. Even when life changed, my interests evolved, or I wasn't sure where I was going, that part of me stayed. I went from drawing on paper to designing digital experiences, from building things with my hands to exploring what I could build with technology, and from wondering how the world works to wondering how I could make it work better.\nNow, we're entering a world where artificial intelligence is changing almost everything: the way we think, work, and create. I don't want to just watch that transformation happen. I want to be part of it. I want to understand technology, design products people love, solve meaningful problems, and build things that didn't exist before. Because for me, design isn't just about making something look good. It's about imagining something better and finding a way to bring it into existence.\nSo, who am I? I'm Veer. Yaswanth Veer. I'm still learning, experimenting, and figuring things out. I don't have every answer, and I don't know exactly where this journey will take me. But maybe I don't need to have it all figured out just yet. Maybe the goal isn't to choose one version of myself and leave the rest behind. Maybe it's to bring everything I've learned, everything I've explored, and everything I'm passionate about into the things I create.\nI'm here to design. I'm here to build. I'm here to turn ideas into something real. And this time, instead of wondering what I might become, I'm going to find out. I'm Veer. And this is just the beginning.","fs":20,"ff":"La Belle Aurore","fw":400,"it":0,"ls":0,"lh":37.05,"lu":1,"ah":"LEFT","av":"TOP"},"hs":"1q5mpz02w5w"},{"id":"349:37563","nm":"_original image 93","ty":"R","w":161,"h":240,"x":1289,"y":2339,"hd":1,"fl":[{"t":"I","ref":"8559b1d08a52081e9d15efa16122206cbcd28d65","sm":"FILL"}],"hs":"1wr3d49vnmg"},{"id":"350:37598","nm":"image 93 (breathe)","ty":"I","w":161,"h":240,"x":1289,"y":2339,"cp":"350:37592","tn":"350:37594","ix":[372],"ch":[{"id":"I350:37598;350:37593","nm":"image 93","ty":"R","w":161,"h":240,"x":0,"y":0,"fl":[{"t":"I","ref":"8559b1d08a52081e9d15efa16122206cbcd28d65","sm":"FILL"}],"hs":"1bdj69yh5um"},{"id":"I350:37598;355:37813","nm":"glint (masked)","ty":"G","w":161,"h":240,"x":0,"y":0,"ch":[{"id":"I350:37598;355:37811","nm":"glint mask","ty":"R","w":161,"h":240,"x":0,"y":0,"mk":"ALPHA","fl":[{"t":"I","ref":"8559b1d08a52081e9d15efa16122206cbcd28d65","sm":"FILL"}],"hs":"1jq700wowre"},{"id":"I350:37598;355:37812","nm":"glint","ty":"R","w":51.52,"h":576,"x":-179.032,"y":-168,"tf":[0.927188,-0.374595,0.374595,0.927188],"op":0.14,"fl":[{"t":"L","hp":[[0,0.5],[1,0.5],[0,1]],"st":[[0,255,255,255,0],[0.5,255,255,255,0.85],[1,255,255,255,0]]}],"hs":"1933ykzy2my"}],"hs":"1zwwtl6r4jq"}],"hs":"4n2ez43e6v"}],"hs":"wmdb26hqkq","cx":12252,"cy":1180}